 validation if there's no negative check. A clean integration of these elements should keep the code robust and user-friendly!", "reasoning_details": [{"type": "reasoning.summary", "summary": "**Exploring CLI Memory Choices**\n\nI’m considering a CLI-based memory using the python-semver 2.10 library. I want to set up the parser with an argument for version information. I’ll also want a subparser to manage different commands, like bumping the version. I need to remember how to handle the potential returns—like checking if args.version returns None—and make sure everything’s structured well. I’ll take my time to get this right; it’s all about clarity!**Designing the SEMVER CLI**\n\nI’m reflecting on the SEMVER CLI implementation I’ve worked on before. In the `process` function, I handle different commands for bumping, checking, or comparing versions. If an unknown command is given, I raise a ValueError. I want to ensure that the main function handles no arguments by defaulting to help. I’m also thinking about how to structure the CLI for robustness, including proper error handling and familiar functionality from previous implementations. This should all keep the user experience smooth!**Addressing TypeErrors in Version Handling**\n\nI’m noticing a TypeError related to invalid keys, specifically one about the `replace()` function receiving an unexpected keyword argument, \"foo.\" It seems that the `VersionInfo` class should handle `replace` with a dictionary update method for better clarity. The `parse_version_info` function needs to pass a properly formatted string, and I want to avoid unnecessary validation if there's no negative check. A clean integration of these elements should keep the code robust and user-friendly!", "format": "openai-responses-v1", "index": 0}, {"type": "reasoning.encrypted", "data": "gAAAAABq1UgSbASquur7lurQubXfID8AyewPPkf2nx6v1-nDpqz_Hg-Ci0a-yttZhjk5CZnGrBwG0_8iJIe6YU-DASrdCpl5eyCJe0VgqXJG5LUA_pFNC8TDpkDfG5LWcuixIu1tUhXCbzStFmrBewij4vM78zbzIe